Playwright Inspector: It provides a proper tool to inspect element to find the locators of the element.

1. Launching the inspector: 

Method 1: Debug Mode

npx playwright test tests/day_31/day_31.spec.ts --debug

This runs the test in debug mode which provides feature of running test step by step.

Method 2: Headless with Inspector (inspect without running a test)

npx playwright codegen https://example.com

This opens the codegen mode, where you can perform the test in the browser and the code will be auto generated for the same.

2. The Inspector Window:

When we launch the inspector via --debug, a seperate window appears with:

a. Current line of the test (highlighted)
b. Action Toolbar (step over, step into, step out, continue)
c. DOM tree showing the page html structure
d. Locators panel showing on which element the current action is taking place
e. Console for runnung quick javascript.

3. The "Pick Locator" feature:

In the inspector window,s top left there's a crosshair (or use Ctrl+Shift+0):
a. Tne browser comes to focus.
b. Move the mouse over to the element you want to locate
c. Hovering highlights the element in blue and shows a suggestion of locators.
d. Click on the element you want.
e. The inspector suggests a locator(getByRole, getByLabel, getByText).

4. Step through debugging:

The --debug method provide some actions that are:

a. Step Over (F10): run current line, see the update on the page, move to the next line
b. Step Into (F11): it current line calls a function, jump inside the function
c. Continue (F5): resumes untill the next breakpoint is reached

5. Inspecting elements in the Inspector's DOM tree:

While paused (or in Codegen mode), the Inspector shows the full HTML tree. Click on elements in the tree to:

a. Highlight them on the live page (blue outline)
b. See their properties (id, class, aria-label, etc.)
c. Get locator suggestions for that element automatically.

